From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 15:59:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] libc: build epoll_pwait2() shim against pre-5.1 kernel
 headers

v261.3 switched the epoll_pwait2() shim from resolving the glibc symbol to
issuing the syscall itself (upstream commit a2de89f622a1, "libc: call
epoll_pwait2 syscall directly instead of resolving glibc symbols"). It now
converts the timeout into a struct __kernel_timespec, which it takes from
<linux/time_types.h> -- a header that only exists since kernel v5.1. The
kernel 3.10 UAPI headers in conda-forge's sysroot 2.17 lack it:

  src/libc/epoll.c:4:10: fatal error: linux/time_types.h: No such file or directory

Fall back to spelling out the struct when the header is missing. Its layout
is part of the syscall ABI (two 64-bit fields everywhere), so this is exactly
what the kernel expects. The syscall number itself comes from the generated
<sys/syscall.h>.
---
 src/libc/epoll.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/libc/epoll.c b/src/libc/epoll.c
index 9bd1645..ac2c232 100644
--- a/src/libc/epoll.c
+++ b/src/libc/epoll.c
@@ -1,7 +1,16 @@
 /* SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later */
 
 #include <errno.h>              /* IWYU pragma: keep */
+#if __has_include(<linux/time_types.h>)
 #include <linux/time_types.h>
+#else
+/* linux/time_types.h appeared in kernel v5.1; spell out the one struct we need for older UAPI headers.
+ * Its layout is fixed by the syscall ABI: two 64-bit fields on every architecture. */
+struct __kernel_timespec {
+        long long tv_sec;
+        long long tv_nsec;
+};
+#endif
 #include <sys/epoll.h>
 #include <sys/syscall.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
